R-PH6 R-R4 R-LK3 R-PH7 R-PH9 R-BL3 R-BL6 R-LF3 R-H6 R-DL5 R-EC13 R-SO7 R-RP2 R-AL6 R-NI5 R-SO19 R-SO20 R-SO22 R-SP1: no compiler download in P0, and `--no-check`. Runs 1 to 4 (slice 009): `update json` on 4 copies of one project, with the compiler `nightly-2026-10-14-38a3029` on PATH. Run 1: the header has the roc pin `nightly-2026-10-09-258ab27`, which is not the roc on PATH. The change needs a probe, the pin names a compiler that is a required download, and P0 refuses it with exit 13 and the message of R-PH7 (no flag name). Talon makes no copy and no probe, writes nothing, and does not download. Run 2: the same header with `--no-check`: no probe runs, so no compiler is needed (R-BL3: --no-check skips the baseline and its download). The header gets the new json and keeps its pin. The output says `check: skipped` (R-BL6). Run 3: a header with no roc pin and `--no-check`: json moves and no pin is written (R-H6). Run 4: the pin is the same other compiler but json is the newest already: nothing to change, so no probe is needed and no refusal comes. Slice 013 (pin gate, G-pin-gate). Run 5 (q5, G5, R-SO19): `talon.toml` has `[toolchain] roc = ">=nightly-2026-10-12-0000000"`, the header pin is nightly-2026-10-14-38a3029 (inside the range), and the roc on PATH is nightly-2026-10-09-258ab27 (below the range). A pin makes the roc on PATH irrelevant for the intent check (`Pin.gate` does not run), and `--no-check` runs no probe, so no compiler is needed (R-SO22): json moves, the pin stays, exit 0. Run 6 (q6): the same header without `--no-check`: the pin compiler is a required download at the first probe, exit 13 with the text of `Compiler.require` (not the text of `Pin.gate`), no write (R-SO22, R-PH7). Run 7 (q7, I1, R-SO20, R-SP1): the pin is the roc on PATH (nightly-2026-10-14-38a3029) and `ROC_TOOLCHAIN` names another id (`toolchain=`): the command holds the pin, prints one note that names both ids and `tln roc use <id>` (the first line of stdout), and the 3 probes of the log run on the pin: json moves, the pin stays. Run 8 (q8, R-SO22, R-H6): no pin, `--no-check`, `ROC_TOOLCHAIN` names an id that is not installed: no probe, so no compiler is needed, json moves, and no pin is written. Run 9 (q9, G2 of slice 024b, R-SO20, R-R4, R-LK3, slice 026): the same as run 7, but ROC_TOOLCHAIN is a link build (link-dev). tln roc use refuses such an id (R-U1), so the note has the second form: it says that a git, link or copy build cannot be a pin and names ROC_TOOLCHAIN and tln roc default, not tln roc use. The probes run on the pin, json moves, the pin stays.
